WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.060
Once you've built a workflow that does
something well, you don't want to rebuild

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.020
it every time you need it. In Sim, any
workflow can become a block inside another

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.600
one. You build it once, then call it from
anywhere, the same way you'd call a

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.460
function in code. It's how small, focused
workflows compose into bigger systems

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:23.800
without turning into a mess. Let me show
you what I mean. This is a workflow you've

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.940
already built. It classifies customer
messages end -to -end on its own.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.700
That whole workflow can fold into a single
block inside another one. You pick which

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.320
workflow it calls and what input it
receives, and then you build around it

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.020
like any other block. When the run reaches
the workflow block, it hands over the

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.060
value, and then it waits for the answer.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.600
The call holds at the top, while the child
workflow runs underneath. The value the

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.680
parent passed in is the child's start
input, and the child runs end -to -end

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.700
with it, the same way it runs on its own.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.420
When the child finishes, its answer comes
back as the block's result, and the parent

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.520
continues with it like any other value.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.160
And the parent can become a block inside
something bigger. Keeping workflows small

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.920
and calling them from each other is how
large systems stay manageable.
